/K [5]
>>
endobj
415 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 219 0 R
/T (Properties of Link-Layer Switching)
/E (Properties of Link-Layer Switching)
/Alt ()
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
416 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 219 0 R
/E (We can identify several advantages of using switches, rather than broadcast links such as buses or hub-based star topologies:)
/Alt ()
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
417 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 740 0 R
/E (Elimination of collisions: In a LAN built from switches \(and without hubs\), there is no wasted bandwidth due to collisions! The switches buffer frames and never transmit more than one frame on a segment at any one time.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
418 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 741 0 R
/E (Heterogeneous links: Because a switch isolates one link from another, the different links in the LAN can operate at different speeds and can run over different media.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
419 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 742 0 R
/E (Management: A switch also eases network management. For example, if an adapter malfunctions and continually sends Ethernet frames \(called a jabbering adapter\), a switch can detect the problem and internally disconnect the malfunctioning adapter. Similarly, a cable cut disconnects only that host that was using the cut cable to connect to the switch.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
420 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 220 0 R
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
421 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 220 0 R
/T (Switches Versus Routers)
/E (Switches Versus Routers)
/Alt ()
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
422 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 743 0 R
/E (Routers are store-and-forward packet switches that forward packets using network-layer addresses.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
423 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 744 0 R
/E (A switch is also a store-andforward packet switch, it is fundamentally different from a router in that it forwards packets using MAC addresses.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
424 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 745 0 R
/E (Whereas a router is a layer-3 packet switch, a switch is a layer-2 packet switch.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
425 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 746 0 R
/E (Switches are plug-and-play.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
426 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 747 0 R
/E (Switches can also have relatively high filtering and forwarding rates.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
427 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 748 0 R
/E (Switches have to process frames only up through layer 2, whereas routers have to process datagrams up through layer 3.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
428 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 221 0 R
/T (Summary)
/E (Summary)
/Alt ()
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
429 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 749 0 R
/E (We saw that the basic service of the link layer is to move a network-layer datagram from one node \(host, switch, router, WiFi access point\) to an adjacent node.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
430 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 750 0 R
/E (all link-layer protocols operate by encapsulating a network-layer datagram within a link-layer frame before transmitting the frame over the link to the adjacent node.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
431 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 751 0 R
/E (A multiple access link is shared among many senders and receivers; consequently, the link-layer protocol for a multiple access channel has a protocol \(its multiple access protocol\) for coordinating link access.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
432 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 752 0 R
/E (we examined error detection and -correction techniques, multiple access protocols.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
433 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 753 0 R
/E (We covered simple parity and checksumming schemes, as well as the more robust cyclic redundancy check.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
434 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 754 0 R
/E (We identified and studied three broad approaches for coordinating access to a broadcast channel: channel partitioning approaches \(TDM, FDM\), random access approaches, and taking-turns approaches \(polling and token passing\).)
/Alt ()
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
